// +gobra

// Package permsintrospect provides *permission introspection*: given a wildcard
// access `acc(x, _)`, the functions declared here name the permission amount that
// is currently held, returning it as a symbolic value `p` about which nothing is
// known except that it is positive.
//
// # Why this is needed
//
// A wildcard permission is enough to read a location, but several Gobra
// operations insist on being given a permission *expression* and cannot be
// applied with a wildcard, even when doing so would be perfectly sound. The most
// common cases in this project are `copy`, `append`, and `range` loops. Before
// this package existed, the codebase worked around the limitation by inhaling a
// fixed permission amount whenever a wildcard was held, i.e. asserting
// `acc(x, _)` and then inhaling `acc(x, R15)` to be able to name an amount.
//
// That workaround assumes strictly more than `acc(x, _)` justifies: it fixes a
// concrete lower bound (here, R15) on the amount held, which nothing in the
// program establishes, and the constant has to be picked so that it does not
// clash with the other permissions to `x` that are floating around. Calling
// `PermIntrospectInt(x)` instead assumes only what a wildcard already means, and
// yields a symbolic amount `p` such that `0 < p` and `acc(x, p)` hold.
//
// # Trust
//
// The functions below have no body; they are axioms. They are semantically
// justified: `acc(x, _)` denotes *some* positive permission amount, so a positive
// `p` with `acc(x, p)` always exists. Viper simply has no way of binding that
// amount to a program variable, which is the only reason why these functions
// cannot be proven within the logic. Because `p` is a fresh symbolic constant
// constrained only by `0 < p`, callers can never derive a concrete lower bound
// from it, and in particular can never turn a read permission into a write
// permission.
//
// Introspecting does not give up the wildcard that was introspected: exhaling a
// wildcard leaves a positive amount behind, so `acc(x, _)` still holds after the
// call, in addition to the freshly named `acc(x, p)`.
//
// # How to extend this file
//
// To support introspection for a type that is not listed below, add a function
// following the same shape and name it `PermIntrospect` followed by the name of
// the pointee type (e.g. `PermIntrospectInt` for `*int`).
//
// This package deliberately imports nothing, so that every other package
// (including `slices`, `definitions`, and the stubs under
// `verification/dependencies`) may import it without introducing an import
// cycle, which Gobra rejects. As a consequence, a lemma whose signature mentions
// a type or a predicate declared in another package Q cannot live here; it
// belongs in Q instead, in a file named `perm-introspect.gobra`.
//
// Whenever possible, such a lemma should not be a new axiom, but should instead
// be *proven* from the functions declared here. That is the case, for instance,
// of `slices.PermIntrospectBytes`, which introspects the permission to a `Bytes`
// predicate instance by unfolding it, introspecting the resulting permission to
// the elements of the slice with `PermIntrospectBytesRange` below, and folding
// the predicate back. Note that this makes `slices` a client of this package, so
// importing `slices` from here would be a genuine import cycle.
//
// The same applies within this file: only the functions without a body are
// assumed. `PermIntrospectBytesRange` is derived from `PermIntrospectBytes`,
// so that the set of things taken on trust stays as small as possible.
package permsintrospect

ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectInt(x *int) (p perm)

ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectBool(x *bool) (p perm)

ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectString(x *string) (p perm)

// Note that `byte` is an alias of `uint8`, so this function covers `*uint8` too.
ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectByte(x *byte) (p perm)

ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectUInt16(x *uint16) (p perm)

ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectUInt32(x *uint32) (p perm)

ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectUInt64(x *uint64) (p perm)

// Introspects the permission to the slice header itself, not the permission to
// the elements of the slice. To introspect the permission to the elements of a
// `[]byte`, use `PermIntrospectBytes` or `PermIntrospectBytesRange` below.
ghost
requires acc(x, _)
ensures  0 < p && acc(x, p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectByteSlice(x *[]byte) (p perm)

// Introspects the permission to a single element of a byte slice. Unlike the
// functions above, which take a pointer, this one takes the slice and an index,
// because that is how access to the elements of a slice is expressed.
ghost
requires 0 <= idx && idx < cap(s)
requires acc(&s[idx], _)
ensures  0 < p
ensures  acc(&s[idx], p)
decreases
func PermIntrospectBytes(s []byte, idx int) (p perm)

// Introspects the permission to the elements of a byte slice in the range
// [start, end), returning a single amount that is valid for *every* element of
// that range. That uniformity is what makes this function usable to introspect
// the permission to a predicate abstracting over such a range, such as
// `slices.Bytes`, from which `slices.PermIntrospectBytes` is proven.
//
// This function is not an axiom. `PermIntrospectBytes` names an amount for one
// element only, and the amounts it returns for two different elements are
// unrelated, so a single call cannot establish the assertion below. The uniform
// amount is instead obtained by introspecting every element of the range and
// keeping the smallest of the results: holding `acc(&s[i], p_i)` for every `i`
// implies holding `acc(&s[i], m)` for `m` the minimum of the `p_i`, which is
// exactly the assertion we are after. The recursion below is what pays for the
// simplicity of the axiom it is built on.
ghost
// the following precondition convinces Gobra that the indexing in the
// quantifiers below is well-formed
requires 0 <= start && start <= end && end <= cap(s)
requires forall i int :: { &s[i] } start <= i && i < end ==> acc(&s[i], _)
ensures  0 < p
ensures  forall i int :: { &s[i] } start <= i && i < end ==> acc(&s[i], p)
decreases end - start
func PermIntrospectBytesRange(s []byte, start int, end int) (p perm) {
	if start == end {
		// no element to introspect, so any positive amount will do
		p = writePerm
	} else {
		pHead := PermIntrospectBytes(s, start)
		pTail := PermIntrospectBytesRange(s, start+1, end)
		p = pHead
		if pTail < p {
			p = pTail
		}
	}
	return p
}
